title: Lung cancer risk factors
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

* ''Key decision:'' Assess tobacco, environmental, treatment-related, and inherited risks; never-smoking does not exclude cancer.
* ''Tobacco:'' Record smoking duration, cigarettes daily, pack-years, and time since cessation.
** Duration matters strongly; pack-years alone can obscure important differences in exposure.
** Offer cessation support; risk falls after quitting but can remain elevated for decades.
** Secondhand smoke also increases risk, including among people who never smoked.
* ''Exposures:'' Ask about residential radon and occupational asbestos, arsenic, chromium, and nickel.
** Asbestos and smoking act synergistically to increase lung carcinoma risk.
** Sustained radon exposure increases risk; assess both home and workplace exposure histories.
* ''Prior treatment:'' Chest irradiation increases subsequent lung cancer risk, especially with continued smoking.
** Review prior breast cancer or lymphoma treatment and reinforce tobacco cessation.
* ''Susceptibility:'' First-degree family history, pulmonary fibrosis, and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease increase risk.
* ''Genetics:'' EGFR T790M detected before targeted therapy warrants genetic counseling and germline evaluation.
** Acquired T790M after EGFR inhibitors usually represents resistance, not proof of inherited susceptibility.
** Confirm suspected inherited variants with germline testing; tumor profiling alone is insufficient.
** Connect confirmed predisposition with [[Cancer genetics]] counseling and individualized family evaluation.

Sources: ASCO SEP 2026, ch. 11, pp. 527–528 (PDF 534–535); NCCN Lung Cancer Screening v1.2026, LCS-1/1A, Discussion MS-6–9; NCCN Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer v7.2026, NSCL-H 3, Discussion MS-18.
